data.content:
The lightest approach for modern .NET (8+) is the built-in **`TimeProvider`** abstraction — no DI container needed. You inject it via a constructor parameter with a default:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In tests**, pass `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (.NET 8+) — no extra production dependency.
- The optional parameter means existing callers don't break.
- `FakeTimeProvider` is one test-only NuGet: `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`.

